This is a Barnes-Hut simulator written on top of Charm++.

To build:
1. Edit the Makefile CHARM_PATH to point to your built copy of Charm++.
2. Edit the Makefile STRUCTURES_PATH to point to the Changa structures library.
   git clone https://github.com/N-BodyShop/utility
   STRUCTURES_PATH = utility/structures
3. make            (GPU force evaluation, needs a CUDA Charm++ and nvcc)
   make GPU=0      (the original CPU force loop)

Usage:
1. Prepare a data file with 10K particles:
   ./plummer 10000 particles.bin
2. Run on a single processor with 100 TreePieces and 50 iterations
   ./charmrun +p1 ./barnes -in=particles.bin -p=100 -killat=50


DATASETS
--------
Three generators, all writing the same format: nbody, ndims and tnow, then
eight floats per particle (position, velocity, mass, softening; the run takes
its softening from -eps, so the last field is only a record of what the
generator thought it should be).

  ./gen <n> <file>                 uniform cube, velocities of order 1e-3
  ./plummer <n> <file>             a Plummer sphere and a copy of itself
                                   displaced by 4, sharing its velocities
  ./galaxy <n> <file> [key=value]  two disk galaxies on a collision orbit

galaxy is the one to use for anything about load balancing, because it is the
only one whose density field both is strongly non-uniform AND moves. Each
galaxy is an exponential disk inside a Hernquist bulge and a Hernquist halo,
in equilibrium (the generator prints 2T/|W| per galaxy, measured by direct
summation over a subsample; it comes out at 0.98 to 1.00), on a Kepler
encounter orbit with the Antennae-like geometry of Toomre & Toomre 1972.
Everything is in N-body units, G = 1, the pair carries unit mass, and the disk
scale length of the larger galaxy is the unit of length.

It prints the parameters the run should use, because they follow from the
model and the particle count and are not guessable:

  ./galaxy 500000 galaxy.bin
  ...
    equilibrium    2T/|W| = 0.990 (galaxy 1), 0.996 (galaxy 2)
    suggested      -eps=0.0249 -dtime=0.005941 -killat=3499

The softening is the mean interparticle separation where the disk is dense,
and the step is the smaller of a quarter of a softening length per unit of the
fastest motion and the free-fall time at the largest acceleration in the model.
Both scale with the particle count, so a 2M-particle file wants a smaller step
than a 500K one.

Note the killat. A resolved encounter takes thousands of iterations; that is
what a collisionless N-body run costs and no choice of initial conditions
changes it. Over the tens of iterations a load-balancing experiment usually
runs, the default dataset is a pair of well-separated galaxies that barely
move. Two ways out, and they trade different things:

  ./galaxy 500000 galaxy-peri.bin steps=60

places pericentre two thirds of the way through a 60-iteration run. The
galaxies then start already interpenetrating, which is dense and moving but is
not a physically prepared encounter -- real galaxies that close have already
been distorted by each other. Alternatively eps= coarsens the force
resolution on purpose, and the step scales with it, so twice the softening is
half the iterations to the same physical time.

Both files run clean here. On four A40s, 500K particles, -p=2048 -b=128
-blockmap=1 with the device walk, 200 iterations of galaxy.bin: the total
energy moves 3.5% over the first 25 iterations as the model settles against
the softened force and then holds to 0.3% over the remaining 175, and the
universe box closes at exactly the orbit's approach speed. The 2M file is the
same model at -eps=0.01569 -dtime=0.003742.

  ./galaxy                         with no arguments, the full parameter list

A highly clustered dataset needs more tree pieces than a uniform one of the
same size, because a piece is a contiguous run of the key ordering and a cusp
puts a lot of particles into a short one. Everything above was run at -p=2048
for 500K particles and -p=8192 for 2M. Too few and the run aborts with "Need
more tree pieces!", which under the default buffering is lost when the abort
follows it -- run under stdbuf -oL to see it.


GPU PORT
--------
The tree walk stays on the host; only the force evaluation moves to the device.

A traversal used to call grav() the moment it decided a node or a remote
particle acted on the bucket it was working on. It now appends that source to
an interaction list and moves on, and one kernel per tree piece evaluates the
whole list at the end of the iteration. This is the shape ChaNGa's GPU port
uses, and it is the only one that gives the device enough work at a time: a
single bucket against a single multipole is a few hundred flops.

Notes on the port:

 * One source type covers both interaction kinds. nodeBucketForce and
   partBucketForce look different but both call grav() with a mass and a
   position -- a multipole is used only through totalMass and cm -- so the list
   is a flat array of point masses and the kernel has one inner loop.

 * A source is tagged with the target bucket it acts on rather than being
   written into a per-bucket list. A tree piece's two traversals interleave:
   doLocalGravity and doRemoteGravity yield to each other every -yield buckets,
   and a remote traversal resumes whenever a deferred node or particle reply
   lands. Comparing each source against the last target turns that interleaved
   sequence into contiguous runs, and each run becomes one CUDA block. Several
   blocks of a launch can therefore target the same bucket, which is why the
   kernel commits through atomicAdd.

 * The particles live on the device for the whole iteration, in the
   DataManager, not in the tree pieces. That is where they already are on the
   host: tree pieces hand their particles to the local DataManager every
   iteration, which sorts them into one array and builds one tree over it, so a
   bucket is a contiguous range of that array. Ordering between the
   DataManager's upload and the tree pieces' kernels is a recorded event, not a
   device synchronize, so the tree pieces of a PE overlap.

 * Every kernel launch happens inside a TreePiece entry method. This is what
   makes CUPTI attribute the GPU time to the tree piece, which is what the
   balancer reads. The last sources of a remote traversal are appended from
   DataManager::recvParticles, so the final flush is bounced through
   TreePiece::finishGpuWork rather than launched where the traversal finished.

 * -b defaults to 128 in a GPU build and 10 in a CPU build. The kernel gives one
   block of 128 threads to a bucket and one thread to each of its particles, so
   a bucket of 10 wastes 118 of them.

 * Forces are not bit-identical to the CPU build. Per interaction the
   arithmetic is the same -- the CPU's sqrt in double, stored to a float, is
   what sqrtf produces -- but a thread accumulates its particle's whole
   acceleration in registers and commits once, where the CPU adds each
   interaction in traversal order.

Three bugs in the stock code had to be fixed for any of this to work, and they
were real before the port too:

 * TreePiece::smallestKey and largestKey were set once in the constructor and
   never reset. They bound the key range a tree piece submitted, and
   DataManager::startTraversal splits the PE's buckets between its tree pieces
   by largestKey; a range accumulated over iterations describes a tree piece
   that no longer exists. From iteration 1 on, the first tree piece on each PE
   was handed every bucket and the rest none. Forces stayed correct; the work
   split did not.

 * doLocalGravity and doRemoteGravity advanced currentBucketPtr past the end of
   their slice of the PE's bucket vector and then read through it. Harmless
   while the value was only stored; not once setContext looks the bucket up.

 * TreePiece(CkMigrateMessage *) was empty, leaving myDM dangling and every
   counter uninitialised. Nothing migrated before, so it never ran.


LOAD BALANCING
--------------
  -lbperiod <N>    run the balancer every N iterations (0, the default, never)
  -firstlb <N>     first iteration after which it may run (default 2)
  -lbasync 1       split the barrier so the next decomposition overlaps the
                   step; needs +LBAsync on the command line, and is ignored
                   with a warning without it
  -lbwindow <N>    instrument only the N iterations before each balancing
                   iteration (default 2; 0 instruments continuously)
  -qd 0            disarm the quiescence deadlock detector

  ./charmrun +p8 ./barnes -in=particles.bin -p=256 -killat=40 \
             -lbperiod=5 +balancer GreedyRefineCentralGPULB

Every iteration ends at a barrier -- AtSync on a balancing iteration, a nop
reduction otherwise -- and the DataManager waits for it before it needs the set
of tree pieces registered on this PE to hold still. It has to wait somewhere:
decompose() sends particles to tree pieces by index and then counts the ones
registered locally, and both are wrong if an element is still in flight. Where
it waits is what -lbasync changes; see ASYNC LOAD BALANCING below.

Migration is nearly free here. A tree piece's whole state is its iteration
counter and where it is in a balancing step: particles arrive from the
decomposition, buckets are handed out by the local DataManager, and the
traversal states are rebuilt every iteration. What moving one accomplishes is
moving its share of the particles, since the decomposition sends by index.


ASYNC LOAD BALANCING (-lbasync=1 +LBAsync)
------------------------------------------
AtSync() is one event: the element stops, and the next thing it hears is that
the whole step -- strategy and every migration it planned -- is over. Under
-lbasync the barrier is split, AtSyncStart() returns without waiting and
AtSyncWait() is where the step is collected, so the two ends of the step become
two events the application can put work between.

There is no work to put there in a tree piece. It has nothing of its own to do
between iterations; the DataManager drives everything. So what overlaps the
step here is the next decomposition, and the split is what makes that legal to
start: the tree pieces report the end of the iteration as soon as they have
joined the step, and the DataManager runs the kick-drift-kick, the universe
bounding box reduction and every histogram round while the strategy runs and
elements move.

One point in the iteration cannot overlap. senseTreePieces() snapshots the tree
pieces registered on this PE and processSubmittedParticles() then waits for
exactly that many submissions, so an element arriving or leaving across it
either stalls the tree build or overruns the vector. That is the whole reason
the unsplit barrier is placed where it is. The split moves the wait to just
before that snapshot instead of removing it: the tree pieces contribute a
second reduction when AtSyncWait() releases them, and the decomposition holds
at DataManager::distributeParticles() until it arrives -- on both PE 0, where
the histogram converged, and the PEs that were handed their ranges.

Whichever of the two arrives second runs the rest, so the cost when there is
nothing to wait for is one branch. Under the unsplit barrier the release is
already set by the time the decomposition gets there, which is why that path is
unchanged.

A balancing iteration prints what the split bought:

  [LBOVERLAP] iteration 7: step finished before the decomposition needed it
              -- fully overlapped
  [LBOVERLAP] iteration 12: decomposition waited 0.014 s for the step

-lbasync needs +LBAsync and falls back to the unsplit barrier without it. Not a
preference: with the flag off, an AtSyncStart() that finds no step due calls
ResumeFromSync() inline *and* returns Continue, so an application written to
the split pattern would report the same iteration twice.


MEASUREMENT WINDOW (-lbwindow)
------------------------------
The balancer should read a short, recent window: opened after the previous
step's migrations have settled, closed at the decision. The whole period
between steps is the wrong thing to hand it -- most of it is stale by the time
it is read, and it includes the step itself.

So instrumentation is off outside that window. The DataManager opens and closes
it on the iteration count, and it does so rather than the tree pieces because a
balancer is allowed to leave a PE with none and the window still has to be
managed there. The closing edge is duplicated in TreePiece::finishIteration,
which is the only place that runs before the decision: the last tree piece's
reportTraversalsDone() merely *starts* the reduction that reaches advance(), so
advance() lands after AtSyncStart and a window closed only there would stay
open across the decision it was meant to bound.

The switch is the same one that gates CUPTI tracing, which is by far the more
expensive half. A window is therefore not only what makes the measurement
meaningful, it is what makes an instrumented run affordable: tracing that no
strategy will ever read is simply not done. With -lbwindow=0 a run traces every
iteration and pays for it.

Under +MetaLB the runtime manages the same switch on its own -- on when
AtSyncWait releases, off in AtSyncSample -- and the calls here are idempotent,
so the two agree rather than fight.


RUNNING
-------
+pe is the total number of PEs, not the number per process, and without an
explicit +pemap each process pins its own PEs starting from core 0, so every
rank lands on the same cores. Give a map that follows the node's NUMA
topology; pemap is indexed by global PE, so one map covers every rank.
